<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Chrysostom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 347–407</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as John the Golden-mouthed, Ioannes Chrysostomus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Chrysostom, John the Golden-mouthed, was a Church Father and Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> around 347. Ordained a deacon in 381 and a priest after 388, he preached in Antioch from 386 to 397, known for his homilies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and his concern for the poor; there too he delivered eight homilies denouncing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and the Christians who took part in Jewish festivals. Made archbishop of Constantinople in 397, he made enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilus-i-of-alexandria">Theophilus I of Alexandria</a> and the empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-eudoxia">Aelia Eudoxia</a> and was banished. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comana-pontica">Comana Pontica</a> on 14 September 407 on his way to further exile, and is honoured as a saint in the Catholic and Eastern churches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom.jpg" alt="John Chrysostom" width="330" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johnchrysostom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The writers of the early church, the Church Fathers, shaped its doctrine: Irenaeus, Origen and Tertullian in the 2nd and 3rd centuries; Athanasius, the Cappadocian Fathers, <b>John Chrysostom</b>, Ambrose, Jerome and Augustine of Hippo in the 4th and 5th.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>It was largely burnt down in the riots that followed the exile of the patriarch <b>John Chrysostom</b> in 404.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its idea was older: at the end of the 4th century <b>John Chrysostom</b> had already described the chant of the liturgy as joining the congregation to the eternal choirs of angels.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cherubikon">Cherubikon (the Cherubic Hymn)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Would you do honor to Christ's body? Neglect Him not when naked; do not while here you honor Him with silken garments, neglect Him perishing without of cold and nakedness.&#8221;</p><footer>Homilies on the Gospel of Matthew, 50 &#183; tr. George Prevost, rev. M. B. Riddle, 1888 &#183; <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/200150.htm" rel="noopener">www.newadvent.org</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For the church is not a gold foundry nor a workshop for silver, but an assembly of angels.&#8221;</p><footer>Homilies on the Gospel of Matthew, 50 &#183; tr. George Prevost, rev. M. B. Riddle, 1888 &#183; <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/200150.htm" rel="noopener">www.newadvent.org</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
